---

<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  category="Technical"
  published={published}
  readTime={readTime}
  slug={slug}
  breadcrumbLabel="Claude Code Skills"
  exploreCategories={["Coding", "Frameworks", "Enterprise", "No-Code"]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <!-- 1. Introduction -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="introduction" class="guide-h2">
      Claude Code Skills, without the jargon
    </h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/tools/claude-code/">Claude Code</a> is strong out of the box. Skills
      are how you make it <strong>reliably good at your</strong> workflows —
      review checklists, deploy steps, test conventions, domain playbooks —
      without pasting the same instructions every session.
    </p>
    <p>
      A skill is a small package (usually a folder with a <code>SKILL.md</code>) that
      teaches Claude a procedure or specialty. Claude can load it when the task
      matches, or you can invoke it with a slash command like{" "}
      <code>/code-review</code>. Unlike dumping everything into{" "}
      <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, skill bodies typically load{" "}
      <strong>on demand</strong>, so long playbooks cost almost nothing until you
      need them.
    </p>
    <p>
      This guide covers what skills are (vs plugins, agents, and commands), how to
      install and organize them, a practical 2026 shortlist, where to discover more,
      and when to build your own.
    </p>
    <InfoCallout title="Data updated: August 2026" variant="info">
      <p>
        Skills packaging, bundled commands, and marketplace UX move with Claude
        Code releases. Treat paths and slash names below as the common 2026 model
        (<code>~/.claude/skills/</code>, <code>.claude/skills/</code>, Agent
        Skills / <code>SKILL.md</code>).{" "}
        <strong>Confirm against current Claude Code docs before standardizing a
        team workflow.</strong>
      </p>
    </InfoCallout>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Quick verdict -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="quick-verdict" class="guide-h2">Quick verdict</h2>
    <KeyTakeaway>
      Think of Skills as <strong>lazy-loaded playbooks</strong>, not as plugins
      or as always-on memory. Put stable project facts in{" "}
      <code>CLAUDE.md</code>; put repeatable procedures in skills; put external
      tools in MCP; put isolated heavy jobs in subagents. People who get the most
      value are teams with <strong>repeated multi-step workflows</strong> and
      power users tired of re-explaining the same process.
    </KeyTakeaway>
    <div class="not-prose mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card-surface p-5 sm:p-6">
        <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-accent">
          High value if you…
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-ink-muted">
          <li>Ship the same PR / release / review ritual weekly</li>
          <li>Onboard others who need the same Code behavior</li>
          <li>Already hit context bloat from a huge CLAUDE.md</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card-surface p-5 sm:p-6">
        <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-accent">
          Lower priority if you…
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-ink-muted">
          <li>Still learning basic Claude Code edit/test loops</li>
          <li>Only run one-off tasks with no repeated process</li>
          <li>Have not written down any team conventions yet</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. What are skills -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="what-are-skills" class="guide-h2">What are Claude Code Skills?</h2>
    <p>
      In Claude Code, a skill is a modular capability package. At minimum it is a
      directory with a <code>SKILL.md</code> file: YAML frontmatter (name,
      description, optional controls) plus markdown instructions. Optional
      supporting files — templates, checklists, scripts — live beside it. Claude
      Code’s skills align with the broader{" "}
      <strong>Agent Skills</strong> open standard used across multiple coding
      agents, with Claude-specific extras (invocation control, subagent execution,
      dynamic context) depending on version.
    </p>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">Skills vs plugins vs agents vs commands</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Skills</strong> — reusable “how we do X” knowledge and workflows.
        Loaded when relevant or via <code>/skill-name</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Plugins</strong> — distribution and packaging. A marketplace
        plugin can install one or more skills (and related config) so you are not
        copying folders by hand.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Agents / subagents</strong> — separate Claude runs with their own
        context. Use them to isolate long side work; they are not a substitute for
        a skill’s written procedure.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Commands</strong> — slash triggers. In current Claude Code,
        custom commands have largely{" "}
        <strong>merged into skills</strong>:{" "}
        <code>.claude/commands/deploy.md</code> and{" "}
        <code>.claude/skills/deploy/SKILL.md</code> both tend to expose{" "}
        <code>/deploy</code>. Prefer skills for anything that needs supporting
        files or auto-invocation rules.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">How skills extend Claude Code</h3>
    <p>
      Base Claude Code can edit files and run tools. Skills add{" "}
      <strong>judgment and sequence</strong>: your definition of a good review,
      the exact migrate-and-smoke order, which tests must pass, what “done” means.
      That turns a general agent into something closer to a trained teammate for
      specific jobs — without burning tokens on those instructions every chat.
    </p>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">Where skills live and how they load</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Personal:</strong>{" "}
        <code>~/.claude/skills/&lt;skill-name&gt;/</code> — available across
        projects on your machine.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Project:</strong>{" "}
        <code>.claude/skills/&lt;skill-name&gt;/</code> — shared via git with
        everyone who clones the repo.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bundled:</strong> shipped with Claude Code (e.g. review, doctor,
        debug-style skills/commands).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Via plugins / marketplace:</strong> installed through Claude
        Code’s plugin flow into the skill locations your setup uses.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Claude discovers installed skills and may auto-apply when the task matches
      the skill description. You can also invoke directly with the slash name.
      Keep descriptions precise so auto-load does not fire on unrelated work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. How to add and use -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="how-to-use" class="guide-h2">How to add and use skills</h2>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">Step-by-step</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Use what is already bundled.</strong> Try{" "}
        <code>/code-review</code>, <code>/doctor</code>, or{" "}
        <code>/debug</code> on a real task before installing anything.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add a project skill for team process.</strong> Create{" "}
        <code>.claude/skills/pr-checklist/SKILL.md</code> (name it for your
        workflow). Write frontmatter + clear steps. Commit it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Or install from a marketplace.</strong> In Claude Code, add a
        trusted marketplace (official Anthropic skills repos are the usual
        starting point), browse plugins, install the skill pack you need. Prefer
        official or well-audited sources.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Or copy a skill folder.</strong> Place it under personal or
        project <code>skills/</code>. Restart or open a new session if discovery
        does not pick it up immediately.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Invoke on purpose the first times.</strong> Call{" "}
        <code>/your-skill</code> or say “use the pr-checklist skill” so you can
        judge output quality before relying on auto-load.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Iterate the SKILL.md.</strong> If Claude skips a step, the skill
        is underspecified — fix the skill, not only the one-off chat.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">Organizing skills</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Project = team truth.</strong> Review, test, release, and stack
        conventions belong in the repo.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Personal = your style.</strong> Commit messages, personal review
        biases, experimental packs — keep these out of the shared tree unless the
        team adopts them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One skill, one job.</strong> “Do frontend” is a bad skill. “Follow
        our design tokens and a11y checklist for React UI” is a good one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Link, don’t paste libraries.</strong> Point at example files in
        the repo; keep SKILL.md short enough to follow.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Version with git.</strong> Treat skills like code: review changes
        that alter release or security behavior.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">Common mistakes</h3>
    <InfoCallout title="Avoid these" variant="warning">
      <ul class="mt-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>
          Installing large community packs without reading a single{" "}
          <code>SKILL.md</code> or script
        </li>
        <li>
          Duplicating the same rules in CLAUDE.md <em>and</em> three skills
          (conflicting instructions)
        </li>
        <li>
          Putting secrets, tokens, or prod credentials inside skill files
        </li>
        <li>
          Expecting skills to replace MCP when you need live tool access
        </li>
        <li>
          Auto-loading vague skills that trigger on every prompt and waste
          context
        </li>
      </ul>
    </InfoCallout>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. Best skills -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="best-skills" class="guide-h2">Best Claude Code Skills (2026)</h2>
    <p>
      Not an exhaustive marketplace dump — a{" "}
      <strong>practical shortlist</strong> of skill types that consistently pay
      off. Names of bundled skills match common Claude Code builds; community
      skill titles vary by pack, so judge by job, not brand.
    </p>

    {
      skillGroups.map((group) => (
        <div class="mt-10">
          <h3 class="guide-h3">{group.useCase}</h3>
          <div class="not-prose mt-4 space-y-4">
            {group.skills.map((s) => (
              <div class="card-surface p-5 sm:p-6">
                <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">{s.name}</h4>
                <dl class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
                  <div>
                    <dt class="font-semibold text-ink">What it does</dt>
                    <dd class="mt-0.5">{s.what}</dd>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Best for</dt>
                    <dd class="mt-0.5">{s.bestFor}</dd>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Setup</dt>
                    <dd class="mt-0.5">{s.setup}</dd>
                  </div>
                </dl>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))
    }

    <KeyTakeaway title="Selection rule">
      Install a skill only if you can name the <strong>repeated task</strong> it
      removes from your paste buffer. If you cannot, write the process down once
      as a project skill instead of browsing for a silver bullet.
    </KeyTakeaway>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Skills vs plugins vs agents -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="vs-plugins-agents" class="guide-h2">
      Skills vs plugins vs agents
    </h2>
    <p>
      Use this when you are stuck choosing an extension type. (Commands are folded
      into the skills column in practice.)
    </p>
    <ComparisonTable
      caption="When to use which extension surface"
      headers={["Skills", "Plugins", "Subagents", "Commands"]}
      rows={extensionCompare}
      highlightIndex={0}
    />
    <p>
      <strong>MCP</strong> is a separate layer: it connects tools and servers
      (browser, DB, internal APIs). Skills tell Claude{" "}
      <em>how</em> to work; MCP gives Claude{" "}
      <em>what</em> it can call. They stack well — a deploy skill might require an
      MCP server for your platform API. See{" "}
      <a href="/guides/mcp-and-tool-calling-explained/">MCP and tool calling</a>{" "}
      and the{" "}
      <a href="/guides/mcp-server-implementation/">MCP server implementation guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Marketplace -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="marketplace" class="guide-h2">Skills marketplace &amp; discovery</h2>
    <h3 class="guide-h3">Where to find skills</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Official Anthropic sources</strong> — Claude Code docs, bundled
        skills, and official skill/plugin repositories registered as marketplaces
        (e.g. Anthropic’s public skills repos via{" "}
        <code>/plugin marketplace add …</code> when documented).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub</strong> — <code>SKILL.md</code>-based repos and “awesome
        skills” lists. Quality varies wildly; star count is not a security audit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Community packs</strong> — multi-agent skill libraries that also
        target Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, etc. Useful for ideas; still audit
        before install.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your org</strong> — private git repos or internal plugin
        marketplaces for company-only runbooks.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">How to evaluate before installing</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Read the full <code>SKILL.md</code> — does it match your stack?
      </li>
      <li>
        List every script and network call; reject unexplained binary blobs.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check maintenance: last update, open issues, who owns it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Install in a throwaway project first; invoke explicitly; watch file and
        shell actions.
      </li>
      <li>
        Prefer project-scoped install for experiments; promote to personal or org
        only after it earns trust.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <InfoCallout title="Trust model" variant="tip">
      <p>
        A skill can instruct Claude to run shell commands and edit files. Treat
        third-party skills like third-party CI scripts:{" "}
        <strong>minimum privilege, read first, pin versions when you can.</strong>
      </p>
    </InfoCallout>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. Recommendations -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="recommendations" class="guide-h2">Practical recommendations</h2>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">Starter skills for new users</h3>
    <ul>
      {starterSkills.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">Worth adding for power users</h3>
    <ul>
      {powerUserSkills.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">When to build your own</h3>
    <p>Build a custom skill when:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The workflow is specific to your repo, compliance, or deploy graph</li>
      <li>You have pasted the same 20–40 lines into chat more than twice</li>
      <li>Community skills almost fit but fight your conventions</li>
      <li>You need templates or scripts that must stay private</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Do <strong>not</strong> build a skill for one-off tasks, for facts that
      belong in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, or for raw tool access that MCP already
      solves. Start from a thin SKILL.md: goal, inputs, steps, definition of done,
      and one example. Expand only when Claude still misses steps.
    </p>
    <KeyTakeaway>
      The highest-ROI skills are usually <strong>yours</strong>: short, opinionated,
      checked into the repo, and tied to a command you actually run weekly — not
      a 200-skill pack you never invoke.
    </KeyTakeaway>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. Related -->
  <section id="related">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">Related guides</h2>
    <ClaudeCodeClusterNav current="claude-code/skills" />
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/claude-code/">Claude Code Guide Hub</a> — full cluster
        index
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/claude-code/plugins/">Plugins</a> package skills;{" "}
        <a href="/guides/claude-code/mcp/">MCP servers</a> give tools skills can
        call
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/claude-code/setup/">Setup</a>
        {" · "}
        <a href="/guides/claude-code/pricing/">Pricing</a>
        {" · "}
        <a href="/guides/claude-code/vs-codex/">vs Codex</a>
        {" · "}
        <a href="/guides/claude-code-vs-cursor/">vs Cursor</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/mcp-and-tool-calling-explained/">MCP and tool calling explained</a>
        {" · "}
        <a href="/guides/mcp-server-implementation/">How to build MCP servers</a>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-sm text-ink-soft">
      Data updated: August 2026. Re-check Claude Code docs for current skill paths,
      bundled names, and marketplace commands before team rollout.
    </p>
  </section>

  <RelatedComparisons slug={slug} categories={["Coding"]} />
</GuideLayout>
